WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.523
Public-key cryptography lets systems that have never shared a secret still communicate securely or verify identity.

2
00:00:07.523 --> 00:00:12.790
At a high level, one key can be public while a related private key remains protected.

3
00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:22.796
That pattern supports key establishment, signatures, certificates, remote administration, secure browsing, software updates, and many other services.

4
00:00:22.796 --> 00:00:28.589
It is one of the reasons modern networks can scale trust beyond a single closed environment.

5
00:00:28.589 --> 00:00:39.121
Several widely deployed public-key approaches depend on mathematical problems that are believed to be difficult for classical computers at appropriate key sizes.

6
00:00:39.121 --> 00:00:50.707
RSA, finite-field Diffie-Hellman, elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman, and ECDSA are examples practitioners see in certificates, TLS, VPNs, SSH, code signing, and identity systems.

7
00:00:50.707 --> 00:01:00.789
The main quantum concern is that sufficiently capable quantum computers could make some of these underlying problems much easier to solve than they are today.

8
00:01:00.789 --> 00:01:05.303
That risk does not affect every cryptographic function in the same way.

9
00:01:05.303 --> 00:01:15.083
Symmetric encryption and hashing are still important, and they are also considered in quantum risk discussions, but the practical concern is different.

10
00:01:15.083 --> 00:01:21.102
Strong symmetric algorithms can often be addressed with key-size and configuration choices.

11
00:01:21.102 --> 00:01:32.236
Public-key migration is harder because it touches protocols, trust stores, certificates, interoperability, hardware, vendors, and long-lived operational assumptions.

12
00:01:32.236 --> 00:01:36.976
The planning urgency comes partly from harvest-now, decrypt-later risk.

13
00:01:36.976 --> 00:01:45.252
An attacker may copy encrypted traffic or stored data today and try to decrypt it later if stronger capabilities become available.

14
00:01:45.252 --> 00:01:58.117
That matters most when confidentiality must last for many years, such as sensitive personal information, government data, intellectual property, regulated records, or strategic business material.

15
00:01:58.117 --> 00:02:10.605
Migration should begin before emergency pressure arrives, because discovering cryptography, coordinating vendors, testing replacements, and updating dependent systems can take a long time.
